i></ul></div></div></div><div class=3D""><div class=3D"entity-content"><p>B=
efore I say anything, please note that I'm usually not for getting too deep=
 into the nitty gritty, exact details of a game to succeed. Especially with=
 a game like this, I like to keep some things unknown as to not be consista=
ntly, nearly perfect until the game changes in some significant way. Also, =
not big into the mathematics... Was one of my worst subjects. :P Haha
</p><p>ANYWAY, my one question is this: The Ideal balance numbers for Genre=
s. What are these numbers? How do they work? At one point, I thought that t=
here was a certain ratio for each genre that held true every time; Lets jus=
t say for an example's sake, that RPGs had a Design/Tech point ratio of 2:1=
 respectively. And it was always this way. To get the optimal score on the =
balance here, you always had to be within the range of 2:1 for RPGs. (With =
this specific RPG ratio under the guidelines of how I THOUGHT the point bal=
ances work.) However, from what I can gather, this page seems to say that t=
he balance is flexible, and isn't a static, never-changing rule, (compared =
to the one in my example.) and is based on the amount of points you get in =
Design and Tech. OR, is it that there is a static Ratio for each genre, but=
 the amount of points you get determines the margin of error you may have, =
yet still be able to optimize your score in the balance area?
</p><p>This is basically the only thing I'm confused about. If someone coul=
d please enlighten me on this, it would be greatly appreciated. Thanks in a=
dvance to those that wish to do so.
</p></div></div></div></div><div class=3D"ReplyList_container__bj-35"><span=
